    playerVisibility = "Low";
}
```

By integrating these elements, you not only enhance the realism of your game but also provide players with a deeper understanding of how air pollution affects daily life. This can be particularly useful in educational games or those set in environments where environmental factors play a significant role.

In the context of game development, the term "evaporation" might refer to the process of reducing an object's properties over time, such as a player's health or resources. For instance, you might implement a system where a player's health gradually decreases over time if they are not actively using it, similar to how water evaporates over time. This could be done with a simple timer or a decrementing value. Here’s a quick example in C#:

```csharp
public class PlayerHealth
{
    public float CurrentHealth { get; private set; }

    public PlayerHealth(float initialHealth)
    {
        CurrentHealth = initialHealth;
    }

    public void DecreaseHealth(float amount)
    {
        // Evaporate health over time
        CurrentHealth -= amount;
    }
}

// Usage in a game loop
playerHealth.DecreaseHealth(1f);
```

This snippet shows how you can simulate the evaporation of health by decrementing it over time. This approach enhances the gaming experience by making the player aware of their current state and encouraging them to take action before their health runs out.

